export default "Period trackers range from simple calendars to full fertility monitoring platforms. The difference matters. Fertility features require daily effort, sometimes specialized hardware, and a tolerance for detailed logging that not everyone needs.\n\nThis assessment helps you decide whether fertility tracking serves your goals or whether basic cycle prediction is enough.\n\n## Question 1: What Are You Trying to Achieve?\n\n**What's the primary outcome you want from tracking?**\n\n- **Option A: Know when my period is coming.** You want a countdown, not a science project. Basic cycle prediction is enough.\n- **Option B: Understand my ovulation timing.** You want to know when you're fertile, whether for conception or avoidance. This requires fertility features.\n- **Option C: Investigate cycle irregularities.** You're gathering data for a healthcare provider about inconsistent cycles, unusual symptoms, or a suspected condition.\n\nOption A does not require fertility tracking. Option B does. Option C may benefit from fertility data depending on what your provider wants to see.\n\n## Question 2: Are You Trying to Conceive?\n\n**Is pregnancy a current goal?**\n\n- **Option A: Yes, actively trying.** Fertility tracking gives you ovulation estimates, fertile window predictions, and a record of timing. This is directly useful.\n- **Option B: No, and I'm using other birth control.** If you have reliable contraception, fertility tracking adds data without changing your behavior. It's optional.\n- **Option C: I'm considering fertility awareness as my birth control method.** This requires dedicated training and consistent daily tracking. The app is a tool, not a substitute for learning the method properly.\n\nOption C carries real consequences. Fertility awareness-based methods require education that goes beyond what any app provides. See a trained instructor or healthcare provider before relying on app predictions for birth control.\n\n## Question 3: How Much Daily Effort Are You Willing to Invest?\n\n**Fertility tracking is daily work. What's realistic for you?**\n\n- **Option A: I'll log my period start and end dates. That's it.** Do not add fertility features. They'll generate noise you won't act on.\n- **Option B: I can log one or two things each morning.** BBT and cervical mucus take under two minutes. This is the minimum for useful fertility data.\n- **Option C: I'm prepared for detailed daily logging.** Temperature, mucus, cervix position, OPK results, symptoms. You'll get the most accurate fertility picture.\n\nIncomplete fertility logging is worse than none. Partial data produces unreliable ovulation estimates. If you can't commit to daily input, stay with period prediction.\n\n## Question 4: Do You Own or Want a BBT Thermometer?\n\n**Basal body temperature is the most reliable retrospective ovulation marker.**\n\n- **Option A: I don't have one and don't want to buy one.** You can still track cervical mucus and use calendar-based estimates, but accuracy drops.\n- **Option B: I have a regular thermometer.** A standard digital thermometer works if it reads to two decimal places and you measure at the same time each morning before getting up.\n- **Option C: I have or want a dedicated BBT thermometer or wearable.** Devices like dedicated BBT thermometers streamline the process. Some sync with apps.\n\nBBT alone doesn't predict ovulation. It confirms it after the fact. Combined with cervical mucus observations, it creates a more complete fertility picture. Without it, you're relying on algorithm estimates based on past cycle length, which are less reliable for irregular cycles.\n\n## Question 5: How Regular Are Your Cycles?\n\n**Cycle regularity affects how useful fertility predictions are.**\n\n- **Option A: Very regular (25-32 days, minimal variation).** Calendar-based estimates are more reliable for you, though they're never precise.\n- **Option B: Somewhat variable (cycles differ by more than 5 days).** Algorithm-based predictions become less accurate. Real-time biomarker tracking (BBT, mucus) becomes more important.\n- **Option C: Highly irregular or I have a diagnosed condition.** Standard fertility algorithms may not apply. Work with a healthcare provider who can interpret your specific pattern.\n\nApps that predict ovulation from cycle length alone are making a guess. That guess is better for regular cycles and worse for irregular ones.\n\n## Question 6: What Will You Do with the Data?\n\n**How will fertility data change your behavior or decisions?**\n\n- **Option A: Time intercourse for conception.** Actionable use. Fertility tracking directly serves this goal.\n- **Option B: Avoid intercourse or add protection during fertile windows.** Also actionable, but requires higher accuracy and more consistent tracking than conception timing.\n- **Option C: Share with a doctor or keep for my own understanding.** Useful for diagnostics. A detailed cycle record with BBT and symptom data gives providers more to work with.\n- **Option D: I'm not sure.** If the data won't change anything you do, it's adding complexity without benefit.\n\nOption D is a signal to stay with basic period tracking until you have a specific reason to add fertility features.\n\n## Your Results\n\n**Review your pattern:**\n\n**Mostly A answers:** You don't need fertility tracking right now. A simple period tracker with accurate prediction and good privacy is the right tool. See [which tracker fits you](/tools/quiz/which-period-tracker-is-right-for-you).\n\n**Option B on Questions 1 and 2, Option B or C on Question 3:** You're a good candidate for fertility tracking. Start with [Floriva's fertility awareness setup guide](/app-guides/floriva-fertility-awareness-method-setup) or review [BBT tracking basics](/app-guides/floriva-basal-body-temperature-tracking).\n\n**Option C on Question 2:** You need fertility education before you need an app. No tracker substitutes for understanding the method. Find a certified fertility awareness educator, then choose your tracking tool.\n\n**Option C on Question 5 or Question 6:** Talk to a healthcare provider first. Your situation benefits from professional guidance, and the tracking tool should be chosen in consultation with your provider based on what data they want to see.\n\nFertility tracking is useful when it matches a clear goal. Without that goal, it's just more fields to fill in.";
